# HELP devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_stale_outcomes_total Publish outcomes refused because they did not belong to a publish the current checkpoint was waiting for. Always zero unless something is wrong; a non-zero value is a bug to report.
# TYPE devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_stale_outcomes_total counter
devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_stale_outcomes_total 0
# HELP devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_window_full_seconds_total Seconds a checkpoint spent waiting for an acknowledgement while another detection was ready to send but the in-flight limit or byte budget had no room. With derivedPublishing.maxInFlight 1 that is nearly all of the publish wait.
# TYPE devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_window_full_seconds_total counter
devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_derived_publish_window_full_seconds_total 0
# HELP devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_duplicate_events_skipped_total Events DETECT skipped because an event with the same identity (tenant, device, type, occurred time, alternate id, payload) was already applied inside the recent window: a duplicate the resolved stream delivered under a second sequence, such as a republish across a rolling upgrade or a residual of envelope settlement. Each is an event that would otherwise have been applied twice. A sustained non-zero rate means something upstream is storing events twice.
# TYPE devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_duplicate_events_skipped_total counter
devicechain_eventprocessing_detect_duplicate_events_skipped_total 0
